INSERT INTO cases (slug, title, domain, difficulty, category_id, company_track, estimated_minutes, scenario, instructions, supporting_data, expected_framework, model_answer, tags, is_published) VALUES (
  'comps-valuation-3', 'What Is Nimbus Health Worth? A Comparables Analysis', 'finance'::domain, 'medium'::difficulty, (SELECT id FROM case_categories WHERE slug = 'valuation'), 'Google', 40, 'Nimbus Health (digital health, US) is preparing for a sale process. You have been asked to establish a valuation range using trading comparables.

**The company.** Revenue of **$173 M**, EBITDA margin of **27%** (EBITDA of $47 M), revenue growth of **6%**, and net debt of **$125 M**.

**Trading comparables.**

| Peer | EV/EBITDA | Revenue growth | EBITDA margin |
|---|---|---|---|
| Peer A — same sector, 3× larger | 10.9× | 11% | 18% |
| Peer B — adjacent sector, high growth | 9.6× | 34% | 13% |
| Peer C — same sector, declining | 8.3× | 3% | 15% |

The banker running the process has proposed simply averaging the three multiples.', 'Produce a valuation range. Provide:

1. **Analysis** — which comparables you would use and why, and the resulting enterprise and equity values.
2. **Risks** — where this methodology could mislead.
3. **Recommendation** — your valuation range and the number you would take to the seller.

Critique the banker''s proposed approach.', '{"target":{"revenue_m":173,"ebitda_margin_pct":27,"ebitda_m":47,"revenue_growth_pct":6,"net_debt_m":125},"comparables":[{"peer":"A","ev_ebitda":10.9,"growth_pct":11,"margin_pct":18},{"peer":"B","ev_ebitda":9.6,"growth_pct":34,"margin_pct":13},{"peer":"C","ev_ebitda":8.3,"growth_pct":3,"margin_pct":15}]}'::jsonb, '1. **Screen the peers** on growth, margin and risk — not sector label alone.
2. **Apply** the defensible multiple range to EBITDA to get enterprise value.
3. **Bridge** to equity value: EV − net debt.
4. **Adjust** for size, growth differential and marketability.
5. **Cross-check** with EV/Revenue.
6. **Present a range**, and say where in it you would settle.', '**The banker''s approach is wrong.** Averaging 10.9×, 9.6× and 8.3× treats three structurally different businesses as interchangeable. Peer B trades at 9.6× because it grows at 20-40%; Peer C trades at 8.3× because it is shrinking. A simple mean smuggles both distortions into the answer and produces a number with no defensible logic behind it.

**Peer screening.** Nimbus Health grows at 6% with a 27% margin. Peer A is the closest analogue on sector and margin, though larger. Peer B is only relevant if this company''s growth is genuinely comparable — at 6% it is not. Peer C sets the floor: it is what the market pays for this sector without growth.

**Valuation.** Anchoring on Peer A at 10.9× and sanity-checking against the others:
- At 10.9×: EV = $512 M, equity = $387 M
- At 8.3× (floor): EV = $390 M, equity = $265 M
- At 9.6× (ceiling): EV = $451 M, equity = $326 M

Note how much the net debt of $125 M matters: it moves equity value one-for-one, and it is the step most often skipped.

**Adjustments.** Apply a size discount against Peer A — smaller companies trade cheaper for liquidity and concentration reasons. In a control sale, offset this with a control premium, which typically runs 20-30%.

**Risks.** Trading comps price minority stakes in public companies on a given day; this is a control sale of a private one. Multiples also compress quickly when sentiment turns, so a range struck today has a shelf life. Three comparables is a thin sample — I would want precedent transactions as a cross-check before going to market.

**Recommendation.** A defensible range is **$265–464 M** of equity value. I would guide the seller to expect the middle of that band, and I would resist the temptation to lead with the Peer B multiple — anchoring the seller on a number the growth profile cannot support is how processes fail late and expensively.', ARRAY['comparables','multiples','ev-ebitda']::text[], true
) ON CONFLICT (slug) DO UPDATE SET
  title = EXCLUDED.title, domain = EXCLUDED.domain, difficulty = EXCLUDED.difficulty,
  category_id = EXCLUDED.category_id, company_track = EXCLUDED.company_track,
  estimated_minutes = EXCLUDED.estimated_minutes, scenario = EXCLUDED.scenario,
  instructions = EXCLUDED.instructions, supporting_data = EXCLUDED.supporting_data,
  expected_framework = EXCLUDED.expected_framework, model_answer = EXCLUDED.model_answer,
  tags = EXCLUDED.tags, is_published = EXCLUDED.is_published, updated_at = now();

INSERT INTO rubrics (case_id, criteria, descriptors, max_score, pass_score) VALUES (
  (SELECT id FROM cases WHERE slug = 'comps-valuation-3'), '{"peer_selection":25,"multiple_analysis":25,"adjustments":20,"recommendation":30}'::jsonb, '{"peer_selection":"Should justify which comparables are genuinely comparable on growth, margin and risk — not just industry label.","multiple_analysis":"Expects EV/EBITDA and EV/Revenue applied correctly, with the bridge from enterprise value to equity value (subtract net debt).","adjustments":"Credit adjustments for size, growth, margin differential, liquidity and control premium.","recommendation":"A defensible valuation range with a point of view."}'::jsonb, 100, 60
) ON CONFLICT (case_id) DO UPDATE SET
  criteria = EXCLUDED.criteria, descriptors = EXCLUDED.descriptors,
  max_score = EXCLUDED.max_score, pass_score = EXCLUDED.pass_score, updated_at = now();

INSERT INTO cases (slug, title, domain, difficulty, category_id, company_track, estimated_minutes, scenario, instructions, supporting_data, expected_framework, model_answer, tags, is_published) VALUES (
  'capital-raise-4', 'Pinecrest Grocers: Raise Capital or Extend Runway?', 'finance'::domain, 'medium'::difficulty, (SELECT id FROM case_categories WHERE slug = 'capital-raising'), 'Stripe', 45, 'Pinecrest Grocers is a grocery business operating in US. It closed last financial year at **$45 M** of annual recurring revenue, growing **42% year on year**.

The company burns **$12 M per year** on a net basis and holds **$15 M** of cash. Gross margin is 64%, and net revenue retention sits at 113%.

The board has been approached by a growth fund offering **$143 M** at a **$715 M pre-money valuation**. The CEO is torn: the round would fund an aggressive push into two adjacent markets, but the founders would take meaningful dilution, and one board member argues the company could reach breakeven on its existing cash instead.

The CFO wants a clear recommendation before the next board meeting.', 'Advise the board. Your answer should provide:

1. **Analysis** — the financial position, computed rather than described. Show your working.
2. **Risks** — what could go wrong on each path, and what you would monitor.
3. **Recommendation** — a specific course of action, with the amount and terms you would accept or reject.

State any assumptions you make.', '{"financials":{"arr_m":45,"growth_pct":42,"net_burn_m_per_year":12,"cash_m":15,"gross_margin_pct":64,"net_revenue_retention_pct":113},"proposed_round":{"amount_m":143,"pre_money_m":715,"implied_dilution_pct":16.7},"derived_hints":{"runway_months":15,"net_new_arr":19,"burn_multiple":0.63}}'::jsonb, 'A strong answer works through, in order:

1. **Runway** — cash ÷ monthly net burn. Here that is $15 M ÷ 1.0 = **15.0 months**.
2. **Efficiency** — burn multiple = net burn ÷ net new ARR = 12 ÷ 19 = **0.63**. Below 1.5 is good; above 2 is expensive growth.
3. **Rule of 40** — growth % + margin %. Compare against the sector.
4. **Dilution** — round ÷ post-money.
5. **Counterfactual** — what breakeven requires: how much growth must be sacrificed, and is that a worse outcome than dilution?
6. **Decision** — commit, with trigger conditions.', '**Position.** Pinecrest Grocers has 15.0 months of runway ($15 M ÷ 1.0 M/month). That is inside the window where fundraising becomes a forced move rather than a choice.

**Efficiency.** Net new ARR is $19 M (45 × 42%). Against $12 M of net burn, the burn multiple is 0.63. This is efficient growth — the company is converting capital into revenue well, which strengthens the case for stepping on the accelerator.

**Dilution.** $143 M on a $715 M pre-money is 16.7% dilution. That is the price of the option on the adjacent markets.

**Risks.** The dominant risk is not dilution, it is raising late. If growth decelerates below 25%, the valuation on offer disappears and the next round is a down round. Against that, the breakeven path requires cutting roughly $12 M of annual spend, which almost certainly means cutting the growth that justifies the current multiple.

**Recommendation.** Raise, but negotiate. The efficiency metrics support the story, so push the pre-money up or take $100 M at the same valuation to reduce dilution while still funding one of the two adjacent markets rather than both. Revisit immediately if net new ARR falls two consecutive quarters, or if runway drops below 12 months.', ARRAY['fundraising','burn','runway','dilution']::text[], true
) ON CONFLICT (slug) DO UPDATE SET
  title = EXCLUDED.title, domain = EXCLUDED.domain, difficulty = EXCLUDED.difficulty,
  category_id = EXCLUDED.category_id, company_track = EXCLUDED.company_track,
  estimated_minutes = EXCLUDED.estimated_minutes, scenario = EXCLUDED.scenario,
  instructions = EXCLUDED.instructions, supporting_data = EXCLUDED.supporting_data,
  expected_framework = EXCLUDED.expected_framework, model_answer = EXCLUDED.model_answer,
  tags = EXCLUDED.tags, is_published = EXCLUDED.is_published, updated_at = now();

INSERT INTO rubrics (case_id, criteria, descriptors, max_score, pass_score) VALUES (
  (SELECT id FROM cases WHERE slug = 'capital-raise-4'), '{"financial_analysis":20,"market_analysis":20,"risk_assessment":20,"recommendation":20}'::jsonb, '{"financial_analysis":"Must compute runway (cash ÷ net burn), the burn multiple (net burn ÷ net new ARR), and the implied dilution at the stated valuation. Credit answers that check whether growth justifies the burn rather than asserting it.","market_analysis":"Looks for comparison against sector benchmarks (Rule of 40, growth-adjusted burn), the funding environment, and what comparable companies raise at this stage.","risk_assessment":"Should identify down-round risk, the consequences of not raising, dilution to founders, and the sensitivity of runway to growth slowing.","recommendation":"Must commit to raise / don''t raise / raise a different amount, with a specific number and the trigger conditions that would change the answer."}'::jsonb, 80, 60
) ON CONFLICT (case_id) DO UPDATE SET
  criteria = EXCLUDED.criteria, descriptors = EXCLUDED.descriptors,
  max_score = EXCLUDED.max_score, pass_score = EXCLUDED.pass_score, updated_at = now();

INSERT INTO cases (slug, title, domain, difficulty, category_id, company_track, estimated_minutes, scenario, instructions, supporting_data, expected_framework, model_answer, tags, is_published) VALUES (
  'dcf-valuation-4', 'Valuing Driftwood Hotels: A Five-Year DCF', 'finance'::domain, 'medium'::difficulty, (SELECT id FROM case_categories WHERE slug = 'dcf'), 'BCG', 60, 'A private equity client is considering acquiring **Driftwood Hotels**, a hospitality business in Southeast Asia. You have been asked to build the valuation case.

The company generated **$702 M** of revenue last year at an EBIT margin of **19%**. Management projects revenue growth of **9% per year for five years**, after which the business is expected to settle into mature, GDP-like growth.

Capital expenditure runs at **5% of revenue** and depreciation & amortisation at **4%**. Changes in net working capital consume roughly **5% of incremental revenue**. The effective tax rate is **23%**.

The company''s equity beta is **1.35**, the risk-free rate is **4.9%**, and the equity risk premium is **4.9%**. Debt carries a pre-tax cost of **7.1%** and makes up **41%** of the capital structure.

The seller is asking **$1269 M** for the enterprise.', 'Value the business and advise on the asking price. Provide:

1. **Analysis** — a five-year free cash flow projection, your WACC, and a terminal value.
2. **Risks** — the assumptions your valuation is most sensitive to.
3. **Recommendation** — is $1269 M attractive? What would you pay?

Show your calculations. State assumptions explicitly where the case is silent.', '{"operating":{"revenue_m":702,"ebit_margin_pct":19,"revenue_growth_pct":9,"capex_pct_of_revenue":5,"da_pct_of_revenue":4,"nwc_pct_of_incremental_revenue":5,"tax_rate_pct":23},"capital_structure":{"equity_beta":1.35,"risk_free_rate_pct":4.9,"equity_risk_premium_pct":4.9,"pre_tax_cost_of_debt_pct":7.1,"debt_weight_pct":41},"transaction":{"asking_enterprise_value_m":1269}}'::jsonb, '1. **Free cash flow** for each of years 1-5:
   FCF = EBIT × (1 − t) + D&A − capex − ΔNWC

2. **WACC**:
   - Cost of equity = 4.9% + 1.35 × 4.9% = 11.52%
   - After-tax cost of debt = 7.1% × (1 − 23%) = 5.47%
   - WACC = 59% × cost of equity + 41% × after-tax cost of debt

3. **Terminal value** at year 5, using either perpetuity growth (g below long-run GDP) or an exit EBITDA multiple. Sanity-check one against the other.

4. **Discount** everything to today, sum, and compare with the asking price.

5. **Sensitivity** across WACC and terminal growth.', '**WACC.** Cost of equity via CAPM is 4.9% + 1.35 × 4.9% = **11.52%**. After-tax cost of debt is 7.1% × (1 − 0.23) = **5.47%**. Weighting these 59/41 gives a WACC of approximately **9.04%**.

**Free cash flow.** Year 1 revenue is $765 M. EBIT at 19% is $145 M; taxed at 23% that is $112 M of NOPAT. Adding back D&A (4% of revenue) and subtracting capex (5% of revenue) leaves a net drag of 1% of revenue, before working capital. Repeat for years 2-5 at 9% growth.

Note the structural point: because capex exceeds D&A by 1 percentage points of revenue, faster growth *reduces* near-term free cash flow. Growth is not free here.

**Terminal value.** With perpetuity growth of 3% (below long-run GDP, which is the discipline that matters), TV = FCF₅ × 1.03 ÷ (WACC − 3%). At these rates the terminal value will represent roughly 65-75% of total enterprise value — which is the honest headline of this valuation: it is mostly a bet on the terminal assumption, not on the five-year plan.

**Sensitivity.** Flex WACC ±1pp and terminal growth ±1pp. That grid will typically swing enterprise value by ±30-40%. Any single-point valuation presented without this range is overconfident.

**Recommendation.** Driftwood Hotels is worth a *range*, not a number. Compare the midpoint against the $1269 M ask: if the ask sits above the high end of the grid, walk away or restructure toward an earn-out that shifts terminal-value risk back to the seller. Given how much value sits beyond year five, I would anchor the offer on the exit-multiple cross-check rather than the perpetuity method, and insist on a sensitivity-driven range in the investment committee paper.', ARRAY['dcf','wacc','terminal-value','valuation']::text[], true
) ON CONFLICT (slug) DO UPDATE SET
  title = EXCLUDED.title, domain = EXCLUDED.domain, difficulty = EXCLUDED.difficulty,
  category_id = EXCLUDED.category_id, company_track = EXCLUDED.company_track,
  estimated_minutes = EXCLUDED.estimated_minutes, scenario = EXCLUDED.scenario,
  instructions = EXCLUDED.instructions, supporting_data = EXCLUDED.supporting_data,
  expected_framework = EXCLUDED.expected_framework, model_answer = EXCLUDED.model_answer,
  tags = EXCLUDED.tags, is_published = EXCLUDED.is_published, updated_at = now();

INSERT INTO rubrics (case_id, criteria, descriptors, max_score, pass_score) VALUES (
  (SELECT id FROM cases WHERE slug = 'dcf-valuation-4'), '{"cash_flow_projection":25,"discount_rate":20,"terminal_value":20,"sensitivity_analysis":15,"recommendation":20}'::jsonb, '{"cash_flow_projection":"Free cash flow must be built from EBIT, not revenue: EBIT × (1 − tax) + D&A − capex − ΔNWC. Penalise answers that discount net income or revenue.","discount_rate":"Expects WACC assembled from CAPM cost of equity and after-tax cost of debt, weighted by capital structure. Credit stating the equity risk premium and beta used.","terminal_value":"Gompertz or exit-multiple both acceptable, but the perpetuity growth rate must be below long-run GDP growth, and the answer should note what share of value sits in the terminal value.","sensitivity_analysis":"Looks for a WACC × growth grid or equivalent, and an honest statement of how wide the resulting range is.","recommendation":"Must land on a value or range and say whether the asking price is attractive."}'::jsonb, 100, 60
) ON CONFLICT (case_id) DO UPDATE SET
  criteria = EXCLUDED.criteria, descriptors = EXCLUDED.descriptors,
  max_score = EXCLUDED.max_score, pass_score = EXCLUDED.pass_score, updated_at = now();

INSERT INTO cases (slug, title, domain, difficulty, category_id, company_track, estimated_minutes, scenario, instructions, supporting_data, expected_framework, model_answer, tags, is_published) VALUES (
  'npv-irr-project-4', 'Meridian Foods: Two Projects, One Budget', 'finance'::domain, 'easy'::difficulty, (SELECT id FROM case_categories WHERE slug = 'npv-irr'), 'Amazon', 40, 'Meridian Foods, a packaged foods company in India, has two capital projects competing for approval and only **₹231 Cr** of approved capital this year.

**Project A — Automation upgrade.** Requires **₹73 Cr** upfront and is expected to generate **₹12 Cr** of incremental after-tax cash flow per year for **6 years**.

**Project B — New production line.** Requires **₹312 Cr** upfront and is expected to generate **₹99 Cr** per year for **10 years**.

The company''s hurdle rate is **13%**.

Two complications. First, the finance team has already spent **₹17 Cr** on engineering studies for Project B, and the COO argues this money "shouldn''t be wasted". Second, Project B''s cash flows depend on a customer contract that is signed but renews annually.', 'Recommend how to allocate the capital budget. Provide:

1. **Analysis** — NPV, IRR and payback for each project.
2. **Risks** — including how you treat the engineering spend and the contract renewal risk.
3. **Recommendation** — which project (or projects) to fund.', '{"project_a":{"investment_cr":73,"annual_cash_flow_cr":12,"life_years":6},"project_b":{"investment_cr":312,"annual_cash_flow_cr":99,"life_years":10},"constraints":{"hurdle_rate_pct":13,"capital_budget_cr":231,"already_spent_on_b_studies_cr":17}}'::jsonb, '1. **NPV** of each project using the annuity form: NPV = CF × [(1 − (1+r)^−n) ÷ r] − I₀
2. **IRR** — the rate at which NPV = 0. Note that IRR alone misranks projects of different scale.
3. **Payback** as a liquidity check, not a decision rule.
4. **Sunk cost** — the ₹17 Cr is spent and irrecoverable. It must not enter the decision.
5. **Profitability index** (NPV ÷ investment) when capital is rationed.
6. **Risk-adjust** Project B for the annual renewal.', '**Project A.** The annuity factor at 13% over 6 years is 4.00. NPV = ₹12 Cr × 4.00 − ₹73 Cr = **₹-25.0 Cr**. Simple payback is 6.1 years.

**Project B.** The annuity factor over 10 years is 5.43. NPV = ₹99 Cr × 5.43 − ₹312 Cr = **₹225.2 Cr**. Payback is 3.2 years.

**The sunk cost.** The ₹17 Cr already spent on studies is irrelevant. It is gone whether or not the project proceeds. The COO''s argument is the sunk cost fallacy in its textbook form, and accepting it would mean letting past spending justify future spending — exactly backwards. The only forward-looking question is whether ₹312 Cr of *new* capital earns above 13%.

**Capital rationing.** With only ₹231 Cr available, both projects cannot be funded. Under rationing the right rule is the profitability index (NPV per unit of capital), not raw NPV and certainly not IRR — IRR systematically favours the smaller project regardless of how much value the larger one creates.

Project A''s PI is -0.34; Project B''s is 0.72.

**Risk.** Project B''s cash flows rest on an annually renewing contract. Renewal risk should be handled by raising B''s discount rate or by probability-weighting the later years, not by ignoring it. A single non-renewal in year 2 or 3 removes most of B''s NPV.

**Recommendation.** Fund the project with the higher risk-adjusted profitability index, and fund it fully rather than part-funding both. Before committing to B, I would make approval conditional on converting the annual contract into a multi-year commitment — that single change is worth more to B''s NPV than any operational improvement in the plan.', ARRAY['npv','irr','capital-budgeting','payback']::text[], true
) ON CONFLICT (slug) DO UPDATE SET
  title = EXCLUDED.title, domain = EXCLUDED.domain, difficulty = EXCLUDED.difficulty,
  category_id = EXCLUDED.category_id, company_track = EXCLUDED.company_track,
  estimated_minutes = EXCLUDED.estimated_minutes, scenario = EXCLUDED.scenario,
