INSERT INTO cases (slug, title, domain, difficulty, category_id, company_track, estimated_minutes, scenario, instructions, supporting_data, expected_framework, model_answer, tags, is_published) VALUES (
  'capital-raise-9', 'Halcyon Bank: Raise Capital or Extend Runway?', 'finance'::domain, 'medium'::difficulty, (SELECT id FROM case_categories WHERE slug = 'capital-raising'), 'Razorpay', 45, 'Halcyon Bank is a retail banking business operating in UK. It closed last financial year at **£86 M** of annual recurring revenue, growing **47% year on year**.

The company burns **£15 M per year** on a net basis and holds **£14 M** of cash. Gross margin is 81%, and net revenue retention sits at 111%.

The board has been approached by a growth fund offering **£127 M** at a **£889 M pre-money valuation**. The CEO is torn: the round would fund an aggressive push into two adjacent markets, but the founders would take meaningful dilution, and one board member argues the company could reach breakeven on its existing cash instead.

The CFO wants a clear recommendation before the next board meeting.', 'Advise the board. Your answer should provide:

1. **Analysis** — the financial position, computed rather than described. Show your working.
2. **Risks** — what could go wrong on each path, and what you would monitor.
3. **Recommendation** — a specific course of action, with the amount and terms you would accept or reject.

State any assumptions you make.', '{"financials":{"arr_m":86,"growth_pct":47,"net_burn_m_per_year":15,"cash_m":14,"gross_margin_pct":81,"net_revenue_retention_pct":111},"proposed_round":{"amount_m":127,"pre_money_m":889,"implied_dilution_pct":12.5},"derived_hints":{"runway_months":11.2,"net_new_arr":40,"burn_multiple":0.38}}'::jsonb, 'A strong answer works through, in order:

1. **Runway** — cash ÷ monthly net burn. Here that is £14 M ÷ 1.3 = **11.2 months**.
2. **Efficiency** — burn multiple = net burn ÷ net new ARR = 15 ÷ 40 = **0.38**. Below 1.5 is good; above 2 is expensive growth.
3. **Rule of 40** — growth % + margin %. Compare against the sector.
4. **Dilution** — round ÷ post-money.
5. **Counterfactual** — what breakeven requires: how much growth must be sacrificed, and is that a worse outcome than dilution?
6. **Decision** — commit, with trigger conditions.', '**Position.** Halcyon Bank has 11.2 months of runway (£14 M ÷ 1.3 M/month). That is inside the window where fundraising becomes a forced move rather than a choice.

**Efficiency.** Net new ARR is £40 M (86 × 47%). Against £15 M of net burn, the burn multiple is 0.38. This is efficient growth — the company is converting capital into revenue well, which strengthens the case for stepping on the accelerator.

**Dilution.** £127 M on a £889 M pre-money is 12.5% dilution. That is the price of the option on the adjacent markets.

**Risks.** The dominant risk is not dilution, it is raising late. If growth decelerates below 28%, the valuation on offer disappears and the next round is a down round. Against that, the breakeven path requires cutting roughly £15 M of annual spend, which almost certainly means cutting the growth that justifies the current multiple.

**Recommendation.** Raise, but negotiate. The efficiency metrics support the story, so push the pre-money up or take £89 M at the same valuation to reduce dilution while still funding one of the two adjacent markets rather than both. Revisit immediately if net new ARR falls two consecutive quarters, or if runway drops below 12 months.', ARRAY['fundraising','burn','runway','dilution']::text[], true
) ON CONFLICT (slug) DO UPDATE SET
  title = EXCLUDED.title, domain = EXCLUDED.domain, difficulty = EXCLUDED.difficulty,
  category_id = EXCLUDED.category_id, company_track = EXCLUDED.company_track,
  estimated_minutes = EXCLUDED.estimated_minutes, scenario = EXCLUDED.scenario,
  instructions = EXCLUDED.instructions, supporting_data = EXCLUDED.supporting_data,
  expected_framework = EXCLUDED.expected_framework, model_answer = EXCLUDED.model_answer,
  tags = EXCLUDED.tags, is_published = EXCLUDED.is_published, updated_at = now();

INSERT INTO rubrics (case_id, criteria, descriptors, max_score, pass_score) VALUES (
  (SELECT id FROM cases WHERE slug = 'capital-raise-9'), '{"financial_analysis":20,"market_analysis":20,"risk_assessment":20,"recommendation":20}'::jsonb, '{"financial_analysis":"Must compute runway (cash ÷ net burn), the burn multiple (net burn ÷ net new ARR), and the implied dilution at the stated valuation. Credit answers that check whether growth justifies the burn rather than asserting it.","market_analysis":"Looks for comparison against sector benchmarks (Rule of 40, growth-adjusted burn), the funding environment, and what comparable companies raise at this stage.","risk_assessment":"Should identify down-round risk, the consequences of not raising, dilution to founders, and the sensitivity of runway to growth slowing.","recommendation":"Must commit to raise / don''t raise / raise a different amount, with a specific number and the trigger conditions that would change the answer."}'::jsonb, 80, 60
) ON CONFLICT (case_id) DO UPDATE SET
  criteria = EXCLUDED.criteria, descriptors = EXCLUDED.descriptors,
  max_score = EXCLUDED.max_score, pass_score = EXCLUDED.pass_score, updated_at = now();

INSERT INTO cases (slug, title, domain, difficulty, category_id, company_track, estimated_minutes, scenario, instructions, supporting_data, expected_framework, model_answer, tags, is_published) VALUES (
  'dcf-valuation-9', 'Valuing Northwind Energy: A Five-Year DCF', 'finance'::domain, 'medium'::difficulty, (SELECT id FROM case_categories WHERE slug = 'dcf'), 'Amazon', 60, 'A private equity client is considering acquiring **Northwind Energy**, a renewables business in Europe. You have been asked to build the valuation case.

The company generated **€723 M** of revenue last year at an EBIT margin of **26%**. Management projects revenue growth of **11% per year for five years**, after which the business is expected to settle into mature, GDP-like growth.

Capital expenditure runs at **8% of revenue** and depreciation & amortisation at **7%**. Changes in net working capital consume roughly **5% of incremental revenue**. The effective tax rate is **30%**.

The company''s equity beta is **1.1**, the risk-free rate is **3.6%**, and the equity risk premium is **6.1%**. Debt carries a pre-tax cost of **8.2%** and makes up **42%** of the capital structure.

The seller is asking **€2548 M** for the enterprise.', 'Value the business and advise on the asking price. Provide:

1. **Analysis** — a five-year free cash flow projection, your WACC, and a terminal value.
2. **Risks** — the assumptions your valuation is most sensitive to.
3. **Recommendation** — is €2548 M attractive? What would you pay?

Show your calculations. State assumptions explicitly where the case is silent.', '{"operating":{"revenue_m":723,"ebit_margin_pct":26,"revenue_growth_pct":11,"capex_pct_of_revenue":8,"da_pct_of_revenue":7,"nwc_pct_of_incremental_revenue":5,"tax_rate_pct":30},"capital_structure":{"equity_beta":1.1,"risk_free_rate_pct":3.6,"equity_risk_premium_pct":6.1,"pre_tax_cost_of_debt_pct":8.2,"debt_weight_pct":42},"transaction":{"asking_enterprise_value_m":2548}}'::jsonb, '1. **Free cash flow** for each of years 1-5:
   FCF = EBIT × (1 − t) + D&A − capex − ΔNWC

2. **WACC**:
   - Cost of equity = 3.6% + 1.1 × 6.1% = 10.31%
   - After-tax cost of debt = 8.2% × (1 − 30%) = 5.74%
   - WACC = 58% × cost of equity + 42% × after-tax cost of debt

3. **Terminal value** at year 5, using either perpetuity growth (g below long-run GDP) or an exit EBITDA multiple. Sanity-check one against the other.

4. **Discount** everything to today, sum, and compare with the asking price.

5. **Sensitivity** across WACC and terminal growth.', '**WACC.** Cost of equity via CAPM is 3.6% + 1.1 × 6.1% = **10.31%**. After-tax cost of debt is 8.2% × (1 − 0.3) = **5.74%**. Weighting these 58/42 gives a WACC of approximately **8.39%**.

**Free cash flow.** Year 1 revenue is €803 M. EBIT at 26% is €209 M; taxed at 30% that is €146 M of NOPAT. Adding back D&A (7% of revenue) and subtracting capex (8% of revenue) leaves a net drag of 1% of revenue, before working capital. Repeat for years 2-5 at 11% growth.

Note the structural point: because capex exceeds D&A by 1 percentage points of revenue, faster growth *reduces* near-term free cash flow. Growth is not free here.

**Terminal value.** With perpetuity growth of 3% (below long-run GDP, which is the discipline that matters), TV = FCF₅ × 1.03 ÷ (WACC − 3%). At these rates the terminal value will represent roughly 65-75% of total enterprise value — which is the honest headline of this valuation: it is mostly a bet on the terminal assumption, not on the five-year plan.

**Sensitivity.** Flex WACC ±1pp and terminal growth ±1pp. That grid will typically swing enterprise value by ±30-40%. Any single-point valuation presented without this range is overconfident.

**Recommendation.** Northwind Energy is worth a *range*, not a number. Compare the midpoint against the €2548 M ask: if the ask sits above the high end of the grid, walk away or restructure toward an earn-out that shifts terminal-value risk back to the seller. Given how much value sits beyond year five, I would anchor the offer on the exit-multiple cross-check rather than the perpetuity method, and insist on a sensitivity-driven range in the investment committee paper.', ARRAY['dcf','wacc','terminal-value','valuation']::text[], true
) ON CONFLICT (slug) DO UPDATE SET
  title = EXCLUDED.title, domain = EXCLUDED.domain, difficulty = EXCLUDED.difficulty,
  category_id = EXCLUDED.category_id, company_track = EXCLUDED.company_track,
  estimated_minutes = EXCLUDED.estimated_minutes, scenario = EXCLUDED.scenario,
  instructions = EXCLUDED.instructions, supporting_data = EXCLUDED.supporting_data,
  expected_framework = EXCLUDED.expected_framework, model_answer = EXCLUDED.model_answer,
  tags = EXCLUDED.tags, is_published = EXCLUDED.is_published, updated_at = now();

INSERT INTO rubrics (case_id, criteria, descriptors, max_score, pass_score) VALUES (
  (SELECT id FROM cases WHERE slug = 'dcf-valuation-9'), '{"cash_flow_projection":25,"discount_rate":20,"terminal_value":20,"sensitivity_analysis":15,"recommendation":20}'::jsonb, '{"cash_flow_projection":"Free cash flow must be built from EBIT, not revenue: EBIT × (1 − tax) + D&A − capex − ΔNWC. Penalise answers that discount net income or revenue.","discount_rate":"Expects WACC assembled from CAPM cost of equity and after-tax cost of debt, weighted by capital structure. Credit stating the equity risk premium and beta used.","terminal_value":"Gompertz or exit-multiple both acceptable, but the perpetuity growth rate must be below long-run GDP growth, and the answer should note what share of value sits in the terminal value.","sensitivity_analysis":"Looks for a WACC × growth grid or equivalent, and an honest statement of how wide the resulting range is.","recommendation":"Must land on a value or range and say whether the asking price is attractive."}'::jsonb, 100, 60
) ON CONFLICT (case_id) DO UPDATE SET
  criteria = EXCLUDED.criteria, descriptors = EXCLUDED.descriptors,
  max_score = EXCLUDED.max_score, pass_score = EXCLUDED.pass_score, updated_at = now();

INSERT INTO cases (slug, title, domain, difficulty, category_id, company_track, estimated_minutes, scenario, instructions, supporting_data, expected_framework, model_answer, tags, is_published) VALUES (
  'npv-irr-project-9', 'Granite Materials: Two Projects, One Budget', 'finance'::domain, 'easy'::difficulty, (SELECT id FROM case_categories WHERE slug = 'npv-irr'), 'Bain', 40, 'Granite Materials, a building materials company in India, has two capital projects competing for approval and only **₹193 Cr** of approved capital this year.

**Project A — Automation upgrade.** Requires **₹76 Cr** upfront and is expected to generate **₹15 Cr** of incremental after-tax cash flow per year for **5 years**.

**Project B — New production line.** Requires **₹246 Cr** upfront and is expected to generate **₹73 Cr** per year for **9 years**.

The company''s hurdle rate is **15%**.

Two complications. First, the finance team has already spent **₹5 Cr** on engineering studies for Project B, and the COO argues this money "shouldn''t be wasted". Second, Project B''s cash flows depend on a customer contract that is signed but renews annually.', 'Recommend how to allocate the capital budget. Provide:

1. **Analysis** — NPV, IRR and payback for each project.
2. **Risks** — including how you treat the engineering spend and the contract renewal risk.
3. **Recommendation** — which project (or projects) to fund.', '{"project_a":{"investment_cr":76,"annual_cash_flow_cr":15,"life_years":5},"project_b":{"investment_cr":246,"annual_cash_flow_cr":73,"life_years":9},"constraints":{"hurdle_rate_pct":15,"capital_budget_cr":193,"already_spent_on_b_studies_cr":5}}'::jsonb, '1. **NPV** of each project using the annuity form: NPV = CF × [(1 − (1+r)^−n) ÷ r] − I₀
2. **IRR** — the rate at which NPV = 0. Note that IRR alone misranks projects of different scale.
3. **Payback** as a liquidity check, not a decision rule.
4. **Sunk cost** — the ₹5 Cr is spent and irrecoverable. It must not enter the decision.
5. **Profitability index** (NPV ÷ investment) when capital is rationed.
6. **Risk-adjust** Project B for the annual renewal.', '**Project A.** The annuity factor at 15% over 5 years is 3.35. NPV = ₹15 Cr × 3.35 − ₹76 Cr = **₹-25.7 Cr**. Simple payback is 5.1 years.

**Project B.** The annuity factor over 9 years is 4.77. NPV = ₹73 Cr × 4.77 − ₹246 Cr = **₹102.3 Cr**. Payback is 3.4 years.

**The sunk cost.** The ₹5 Cr already spent on studies is irrelevant. It is gone whether or not the project proceeds. The COO''s argument is the sunk cost fallacy in its textbook form, and accepting it would mean letting past spending justify future spending — exactly backwards. The only forward-looking question is whether ₹246 Cr of *new* capital earns above 15%.

**Capital rationing.** With only ₹193 Cr available, both projects cannot be funded. Under rationing the right rule is the profitability index (NPV per unit of capital), not raw NPV and certainly not IRR — IRR systematically favours the smaller project regardless of how much value the larger one creates.

Project A''s PI is -0.34; Project B''s is 0.42.

**Risk.** Project B''s cash flows rest on an annually renewing contract. Renewal risk should be handled by raising B''s discount rate or by probability-weighting the later years, not by ignoring it. A single non-renewal in year 2 or 3 removes most of B''s NPV.

**Recommendation.** Fund the project with the higher risk-adjusted profitability index, and fund it fully rather than part-funding both. Before committing to B, I would make approval conditional on converting the annual contract into a multi-year commitment — that single change is worth more to B''s NPV than any operational improvement in the plan.', ARRAY['npv','irr','capital-budgeting','payback']::text[], true
) ON CONFLICT (slug) DO UPDATE SET
  title = EXCLUDED.title, domain = EXCLUDED.domain, difficulty = EXCLUDED.difficulty,
  category_id = EXCLUDED.category_id, company_track = EXCLUDED.company_track,
  estimated_minutes = EXCLUDED.estimated_minutes, scenario = EXCLUDED.scenario,
  instructions = EXCLUDED.instructions, supporting_data = EXCLUDED.supporting_data,
  expected_framework = EXCLUDED.expected_framework, model_answer = EXCLUDED.model_answer,
  tags = EXCLUDED.tags, is_published = EXCLUDED.is_published, updated_at = now();

INSERT INTO rubrics (case_id, criteria, descriptors, max_score, pass_score) VALUES (
  (SELECT id FROM cases WHERE slug = 'npv-irr-project-9'), '{"financial_analysis":30,"assumptions":20,"risk_assessment":20,"recommendation":30}'::jsonb, '{"financial_analysis":"NPV must be computed with the correct discount rate and sign convention. Credit IRR and payback as supporting metrics, and penalise ranking projects on IRR alone when scale differs.","assumptions":"Should state assumptions about the discount rate, project life, and terminal/salvage value, and flag that sunk costs are irrelevant.","risk_assessment":"Expects a break-even or sensitivity view: what has to be true for NPV to go negative.","recommendation":"A go/no-go under capital rationing, with a clear ranking rule."}'::jsonb, 100, 60
) ON CONFLICT (case_id) DO UPDATE SET
  criteria = EXCLUDED.criteria, descriptors = EXCLUDED.descriptors,
  max_score = EXCLUDED.max_score, pass_score = EXCLUDED.pass_score, updated_at = now();

INSERT INTO cases (slug, title, domain, difficulty, category_id, company_track, estimated_minutes, scenario, instructions, supporting_data, expected_framework, model_answer, tags, is_published) VALUES (
  'ma-accretion-9', 'Ferro Industries Acquires a Rival: Accretive or Not?', 'finance'::domain, 'medium'::difficulty, (SELECT id FROM case_categories WHERE slug = 'mergers-acquisitions'), 'Morgan Stanley', 55, 'Ferro Industries (industrial components, Europe) is considering acquiring a smaller competitor.

**Acquirer.** Net income of **€101 M**, **62 million shares** outstanding, trading at **€100** per share. Current EPS is therefore **€1.63**.

**Target.** Net income of **€21 M**, currently valued by the market at **€703 M**. The board expects to pay a **25% premium** to that price.

Management projects **€54 M** of annual run-rate synergies, roughly 78% of which are cost synergies from overlapping sales and back-office functions, with the remainder from cross-selling. One-time integration costs are estimated at **€52 M**.

The deal would be financed with debt at **7.4%**. The marginal tax rate is 25%.', 'Advise the board on whether to proceed. Provide:

1. **Analysis** — accretion/dilution to EPS in year one and at full synergy run-rate.
2. **Risks** — what would make this deal destroy value.
3. **Recommendation** — proceed, renegotiate, or walk. Specify price and structure.', '{"acquirer":{"net_income_m":101,"shares_outstanding":62,"share_price":100,"eps":1.63},"target":{"net_income_m":21,"market_value_m":703,"premium_pct":25},"deal":{"run_rate_synergies_m":54,"integration_cost_m":52,"debt_rate_pct":7.4,"tax_rate_pct":25}}'::jsonb, '1. **Purchase price** = market value × (1 + premium).
2. **Financing cost** = purchase price × debt rate, then after tax.
3. **Combined earnings** = acquirer + target + after-tax synergies − after-tax interest.
4. **Combined EPS** = combined earnings ÷ share count (unchanged in an all-cash deal).
5. **Compare** against standalone EPS. Do this for year 1 (partial synergies, integration costs) and steady state.
6. **Sanity check** the price against the target''s standalone value.', '**Purchase price.** €703 M × 1.25 = **€879 M**.

**Financing.** At 7.4%, annual interest is €65.0 M, or €48.8 M after tax.

**Steady-state EPS.** Combined earnings = 101 + 21 + 40.5 (after-tax synergies) − 48.8 (after-tax interest) = **€113.7 M**.

Share count is unchanged in an all-cash deal, so EPS = 1.83 versus 1.63 standalone. That is **accretive**.

**The load-bearing assumption.** Strip the synergies out and the deal is clearly dilutive. In other words, the entire case rests on delivering €54 M of synergies. Year one will be worse than steady state: synergies phase in over 18-24 months while the €52 M of integration costs land immediately.

**Risks.** Cost synergies from overlapping back-office functions are the credible portion and are within management''s control. The cross-selling component is the part that routinely fails to appear — it depends on customers behaving as the model assumes. I would haircut revenue synergies by at least half in the base case. Beyond that: integration distraction during the transition, key-person attrition at the target, and the fact that floating-rate debt makes accretion sensitive to rates moving against you.

**Recommendation.** Accretion here is thin and synergy-dependent, so the discipline has to come from price and structure rather than optimism. I would cap the premium below 25% — every point of premium is paid with certainty while every point of synergy is a forecast — and push for part-stock consideration so the seller shares integration risk. Walk away if the cost-synergy diligence cannot substantiate at least 32 M of the €54 M from headcount and facilities overlap alone.', ARRAY['m&a','accretion-dilution','synergies','deal-structure']::text[], true
) ON CONFLICT (slug) DO UPDATE SET
  title = EXCLUDED.title, domain = EXCLUDED.domain, difficulty = EXCLUDED.difficulty,
  category_id = EXCLUDED.category_id, company_track = EXCLUDED.company_track,
  estimated_minutes = EXCLUDED.estimated_minutes, scenario = EXCLUDED.scenario,
  instructions = EXCLUDED.instructions, supporting_data = EXCLUDED.supporting_data,
