INSERT INTO cases (slug, title, domain, difficulty, category_id, company_track, estimated_minutes, scenario, instructions, supporting_data, expected_framework, model_answer, tags, is_published) VALUES (
  'growth-strategy-14', 'Bluepeak Logistics: Finding 12% Growth', 'consulting'::domain, 'medium'::difficulty, (SELECT id FROM case_categories WHERE slug = 'growth-strategy'), 'Goldman Sachs', 45, 'Bluepeak Logistics (logistics, Southeast Asia) has grown at **5% a year** for three years. The new CEO has committed the board to **12% growth** — a gap of roughly **$74 M** of incremental revenue in year one.

**What we know.**
- Current revenue: **$1052 M**
- **4559** active customers
- Annual customer churn: **13%**
- Estimated share of existing customers'' relevant spend: **31%**
- The core market is growing at 3% — so the company is roughly holding share

The CEO''s instinct is to enter a new geography. The CFO thinks the answer is in the existing base. Nobody has yet sized either.', 'Build the growth case. Provide:

1. **Analysis** — where growth could come from, sized.
2. **Risks** — of the options you recommend and reject.
3. **Recommendation** — a prioritised growth portfolio for the next 24 months.', '{"current_state":{"revenue_m":1052,"growth_pct":5,"target_growth_pct":12,"revenue_gap_m":74},"customers":{"active_customers":4559,"annual_churn_pct":13,"share_of_wallet_pct":31,"average_revenue_per_customer":230752},"market":{"core_market_growth_pct":3}}'::jsonb, 'Decompose growth into five sources and size each:
1. **Retention** — reducing 13% churn
2. **Penetration** — raising 31% share of wallet
3. **New customers** in existing segments
4. **New products** to existing customers
5. **New geographies or segments**

Score on size, speed, cost and risk. The first two are usually cheapest and fastest; the last is usually slowest.', '**The gap.** $74 M of incremental revenue in year one.

**Source 1 — retention.** Churn of 13% destroys about $137 M of revenue annually. Cutting churn by a third recovers $46 M — **62% of the gap**, from customers who already chose the product once. This is almost always the cheapest growth available and the least celebrated, because it does not look like growth on a slide.

**Source 2 — share of wallet.** At 31% of relevant customer spend, 69% is going elsewhere. Moving to 36% adds roughly $170 M. The infrastructure exists — these are known customers with known buyers.

**Source 3 — new customers.** At current average revenue per customer of $230.8k, closing the gap this way alone needs about 321 new customers — 7% growth in the customer base in a market growing at low single digits. That means taking share, which is slower and more expensive than the plan assumes.

**Sources 4 and 5 — new products and geographies.** Both are real but neither pays inside 24 months. New geography in particular carries an 18-month lead time before meaningful revenue, and consumes disproportionate management attention.

**On the CEO''s instinct.** New geography is the least likely of the five to close a *year-one* gap, and it is the most expensive way to discover you were wrong. The CFO is closer to right: the existing base holds 291% of the required growth at a fraction of the cost and risk.

**Recommendation — a portfolio, sequenced.**

- **Months 0-6: retention.** Instrument churn by cohort and reason, then fix the top two causes. Fastest payback, and it compounds into every later initiative.
- **Months 0-12: penetration.** Build an account-plan motion for the top 20% of customers by potential. Existing relationships, no new acquisition cost.
- **Months 6-18: new customer acquisition** in the two best-performing existing segments, funded by the margin the first two workstreams release.
- **Months 12-24: one adjacency**, piloted — not a geography and a product line simultaneously.

**Risks.** The main one is that 12% may simply not be achievable in year one in a market growing at low single digits, and committing the board to it invites the wrong behaviours — discounting to hit a number, or acquisitions made under time pressure. I would re-contract with the board on a two-year path to 12% rather than a one-year leap, and report the retention and share-of-wallet metrics as leading indicators so progress is visible before the revenue arrives.', ARRAY['growth','adjacency','portfolio']::text[], true
) ON CONFLICT (slug) DO UPDATE SET
  title = EXCLUDED.title, domain = EXCLUDED.domain, difficulty = EXCLUDED.difficulty,
  category_id = EXCLUDED.category_id, company_track = EXCLUDED.company_track,
  estimated_minutes = EXCLUDED.estimated_minutes, scenario = EXCLUDED.scenario,
  instructions = EXCLUDED.instructions, supporting_data = EXCLUDED.supporting_data,
  expected_framework = EXCLUDED.expected_framework, model_answer = EXCLUDED.model_answer,
  tags = EXCLUDED.tags, is_published = EXCLUDED.is_published, updated_at = now();

INSERT INTO rubrics (case_id, criteria, descriptors, max_score, pass_score) VALUES (
  (SELECT id FROM cases WHERE slug = 'growth-strategy-14'), '{"problem_structuring":25,"option_generation":25,"evaluation":25,"recommendation":25}'::jsonb, '{"problem_structuring":"Expects growth decomposed systematically — existing vs new customers, existing vs new products, existing vs new geographies.","option_generation":"Several genuinely distinct options, not variations of one.","evaluation":"Options scored against a stated set of criteria, with numbers where available.","recommendation":"A prioritised portfolio with sequencing and resourcing."}'::jsonb, 100, 60
) ON CONFLICT (case_id) DO UPDATE SET
  criteria = EXCLUDED.criteria, descriptors = EXCLUDED.descriptors,
  max_score = EXCLUDED.max_score, pass_score = EXCLUDED.pass_score, updated_at = now();

INSERT INTO cases (slug, title, domain, difficulty, category_id, company_track, estimated_minutes, scenario, instructions, supporting_data, expected_framework, model_answer, tags, is_published) VALUES (
  'market-sizing-14', 'Market Sizing: Electric two-wheelers in UK', 'consulting'::domain, 'medium'::difficulty, (SELECT id FROM case_categories WHERE slug = 'market-sizing'), 'Google', 25, 'Halcyon Bank is considering launching **electric two-wheelers** in UK and needs a defensible estimate of the annual market before committing to a business plan.

There is no reliable published market research for this category in UK. You have roughly 267 million people in the addressable region, a broadly typical income distribution for the geography, and no proprietary data.

The investment committee does not expect precision. It expects a number it can interrogate, with every assumption visible and challengeable.', 'Estimate the annual market size for electric two-wheelers in UK. Provide:

1. **Analysis** — your structure, assumptions and arithmetic.
2. **Risks** — which assumptions your answer is most sensitive to.
3. **Recommendation** — your estimate, as a range, and how you would validate it.

State every assumption. Round aggressively — precision here is false comfort.', '{"context":{"product":"electric two-wheelers","geography":"UK","addressable_population_millions":267},"note":"No published market data. Estimate from first principles."}'::jsonb, '**Bottom-up** is usually more defensible:

Population → filter to the relevant buying unit (households, businesses, vehicles) → penetration rate → purchase frequency or replacement cycle → average price → annual market value.

Then **cross-check top-down**: total category spend in the geography × the share this product could represent.

If the two land within a factor of two, the estimate is usable. If not, one assumption is wrong — find it.', '**Structure.** I will size this bottom-up and cross-check top-down.

**Bottom-up.**
1. Addressable population: ~267 million.
2. Convert to buying units. For a consumer product, 267 million people at ~4 people per household gives ~67 million households. For a business product, the relevant unit is establishments, not people — a different tree entirely, and I would state which I am using up front.
3. Filter to the addressable segment. Assume ~24% of units are plausible buyers on income, need or regulation.
4. Apply penetration. For an established category assume 37%; for an emerging one, single digits. electric two-wheelers is emerging in this market, so I will use the lower end and flag it as the most sensitive assumption.
5. Apply a replacement cycle. A durable good bought every 4 years means only ~25% of the installed base buys in any given year.
6. Multiply by average price.

**Cross-check.** Total consumer or business spend in the relevant parent category × the share this sub-category plausibly represents. If bottom-up and top-down differ by more than 2×, one of the assumptions is wrong — most often penetration or the replacement cycle.

**Sensitivity.** The answer swings hardest on penetration and price. Halving penetration halves the market; the population figure barely matters by comparison. This is the useful output of the exercise: it tells the investment committee which single number to go and research first, rather than pretending the whole estimate is equally uncertain.

**Recommendation.** Present a **range**, not a point — typically the bottom-up estimate ±50%. Then validate the two load-bearing assumptions cheaply before committing: 30 customer interviews will pin down penetration and willingness to pay far better than another week of desk research. A market size presented as a single number invites false confidence; a range with named uncertainties invites the right next question.', ARRAY['sizing','estimation','assumptions']::text[], true
) ON CONFLICT (slug) DO UPDATE SET
  title = EXCLUDED.title, domain = EXCLUDED.domain, difficulty = EXCLUDED.difficulty,
  category_id = EXCLUDED.category_id, company_track = EXCLUDED.company_track,
  estimated_minutes = EXCLUDED.estimated_minutes, scenario = EXCLUDED.scenario,
  instructions = EXCLUDED.instructions, supporting_data = EXCLUDED.supporting_data,
  expected_framework = EXCLUDED.expected_framework, model_answer = EXCLUDED.model_answer,
